WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.580
In 1991, after several years of negotiations
and a whole lot of tension, the Baltic States:

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:19.070
Lithuania, Latvia, and Estonia finally managed
to break away from the Soviet Union.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.740
The finally had the freedom that they had
wanted for so long.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:43.680
Since then, 50 years have passed by since
the soldiers of the Red Army occupied these

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.680
countries.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.430
But during the years under the Soviets, repression
and fear were the basic ingredients in the

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.610
lives of people in Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.320
Let’s have a look at some pretty alarming
data.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.110
In Lithuania alone, a country of little more
than 2.5 million people, these numbers were

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:08.280
recorded:
(200.000 people were arrested, questioned

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.840
and imprisoned, 132.000 people were deported,
especially to Siberia.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.740
75.000 prisoners, deportees and partisans
were eliminated by the Soviet regime.)

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.799
And if all that wasn’t enough, at the time
of their independance, poverty and shortages

00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:28.170
were every day realities for all the inhabitants
of these countries.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:33.549
“I remember the days, when the counters
of the shops in Tallinn were empty and the

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:34.840
people were starving.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.899
The economy more or less had to be rebuilt
from scratch".

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:40.219
Since then, dear viewer, things have changed
a lot.

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:42.400
More than a lot in fact…

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.289
From the very moment these countries regained
their independence, they decided to bet it

00:01:47.289 --> 00:01:53.090
all on democracy and freedom.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.840
Their economies are today among the freest
ones, they are members of the EU and NATO,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.069
and they prosper.

00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:02.120
Today these are countries with really high
standards of living.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.539
&nbsp;
They can really now be considered rich countries.

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:08.200
&nbsp;
The transformation has been total and remarkable.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.620
In 2009, they suffered, along with the rest
of the world, through the financial crisis

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.800
and it really seemed as if their economies
were on the verge of collapse...

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.910
“Latvia’s economy contracts almost 18
percent in Q4 2009”.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:24.910
Seekingalpha

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.510
But, against all odds, they managed to recover
in a very short period of time.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:34.040
Indeed, they became an example to other countries
like Spain, Greece and Portugal.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.980
&nbsp;
But that’s a whole different thing that

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.100
we’re going to talk about in a future video.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:43.140
In this video we are going to focus on Lithuania
talk about a problem that is shared by a lot

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.720
of countries in Eastern Europe.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:54.540
A problem that, today, is actually one of
the biggest threats to their future…

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.540
BRAIN DRAIN

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:01.700
The history of Lithuania, and all the Baltic
States in general, since their freedom in

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.510
1991, is a history of success.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.370
&nbsp;
Today they are modern, open countries with

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.830
prosperous economies.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.900
But… as with any good story…

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.880
not everything is quite so pretty.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.840
Yes, there are issues these countries are
facing, and today we are going to particularly

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.680
focus on Lithuania.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.790
In this case, the biggest problem is “brain
drain”.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.760
Yep, emigration with an “E”!

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.450
And this is no small problem for the country…

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:34.110
Since 2004 only, when the country was integrated
in the EU, Lithuania has lost more than 10%

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.580
of its population.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:41.260
That is: more than 300,000 people, especially
young people, have decided to pack up and

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.570
move to a different country.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.720
And, by the way, most of them have chosen
the United Kingdom.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.890
Yes, while in Western Europe and the United
States they are concerned about immigration,

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:56.700
in Lithuania it is the other way around - they
are concerned about people leaving.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.480
They are worried that this might end up decapitalizing
the country.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.430
“Since 1990 we have lost about 800,000 people
and for a small country this is a huge loss.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:10.900
We are losing our youngest and brightest so
in the future we will just have old people

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.100
who will be dying.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.130
We will have a dying society.”

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:18.810
And, as always, the ones who are leaving tend
to be the most capable and best prepared young

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:19.810
people.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.870
This, of course, has an significant impact
on country’s economy: the number of young

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.830
people working decreases, talent leaves the
country, and the population ages.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:33.479
But this isn’t just a problem for Lithuania…

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:40.439
“Since the early 1990s, some 20 million
of central and eastern Europe’s most talented

00:04:40.439 --> 00:04:41.729
workers have left.

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:43.680
And they have largely headed for Western Europe”.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.680
Foreign Affairs.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.110
So who you think has benefitted the most from
all this talent redistribution?

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.679
Yes, that’s right, the wealthiest countries…

00:04:50.679 --> 00:04:52.710
&nbsp;
And that is why we have to be very careful

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.710
about the messages being sent by politicians
who are attempting to criminalize immigration

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.129
in order to get votes.

00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:02.190
Yep, we’re looking at you Theresa May and
Donald Trump.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:03.190
But…

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:07.550
I know what you are thinking: If the Baltic
countries have been so successful…Why are

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:08.550
people leaving?

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.580
Well, because they are looking out for their
own personal success...

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:16.840
Look, for one, these countries have managed
to develop a well prepared young population

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.240
who can also speak English (two must-haves
when it comes to look for a future in other

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.240
places).

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.639
&nbsp;
And then we also have the fact that because

00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:28.379
of all the growth that has happened since
1991, these people can now leave the country

00:05:28.379 --> 00:05:29.400
in a comfortable way.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.150
But, there is still a key question to answer:
Why are they leaving if things are going so

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.150
well!?

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:40.819
Well, that isn’t hard to explain…

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:47.130
Even though the salaries have improved a lot
in the last years, the difference with the

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.870
wealthiest countries is still quite big.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:56.029
As an example, while the average salary in
Lithuania is about 800€, in the United Kingdom

00:05:56.029 --> 00:06:00.210
it is almost 2,500€, three times more.

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:05.010
All of this might leave you wondering whether
this impressive economic development was actually

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.389
good for the Baltic countries.

00:06:06.389 --> 00:06:11.650
Well, we’d say that yes it’s still worth
it, but it does leave us with a couple of

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:17.029
very important questions: First, “What is
the cost of this?” and second “How can

00:06:17.029 --> 00:06:21.620
they stop the brain drain?”

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.830
PUTTING AN END TO EMIGRATION

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:28.860
So, stopping this brain drain has become a
major priority for Eastern European countries.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.819
In some of these countries like Poland, Latvia
and Hungary they have started a return campaign

00:06:33.819 --> 00:06:37.590
in order to persuade emigrants to come back
home.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:41.139
"I would welcome all Polish people who went
abroad.

00:06:41.139 --> 00:06:44.289
If only they could come back, it would be
a great day for Poland".

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:46.439
Mateusz Morawiecki, Polish deputy Prime Minister.

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:48.930
&nbsp;
However, while this is a nice sentiment, it

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:53.090
is definitely in Lithuania, where there is
a larger concern about the brain drain, that

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:58.639
there has been more significant support from
society.

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:06.979
(In the last parliamentary elections celebrated
in October, the Lithuanian Greens and Farmers

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:10.589
Union won the elections and took control of
the government.

00:07:10.589 --> 00:07:14.370
This pintoresque party had only had one MP
in the previous elections.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.569
Their &nbsp;main promise: putting an end on emigration.)

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:22.020
But, of course, it is easier said than done.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.689
So the question is: How is this party going
to succeed - a party led, by the way, by Ramunas

00:07:26.689 --> 00:07:30.110
Karbauskis one of the most important businessmen
in the country?

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.330
&nbsp;
Well, apparently their top priority is to

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:35.819
stop emigration by attracting much more foreign
investment.

00:07:35.819 --> 00:07:39.759
They claim that, if they can succeed in getting
more foreign money for investment, they will

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:42.879
be able to provide more jobs with higher salaries...

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:45.409
&nbsp;
So, how are they planning to do this?

00:07:45.409 --> 00:07:50.270
Well, by reducing bureaucracy, chasing corruption
and, also, by making adjustments to the labour

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.270
market.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.520
&nbsp;
And the truth is that this does make a ton

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:53.520
of sense.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.740
We could say that, once again, the Baltic
countries really seem to know how to get things

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:57.740
done.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:01.229
But… hold on for just a second… because
this isn't the end of the story.

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:03.240
There are some other things that…

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:04.240
well…

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.610
they don’t look quite so good…

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:11.610
THE SHADOWS OF THE LITHUANIAN PLAN

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:17.720
On the one hand, at the same time as the government
promised foreign investment, they have also

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.839
committed to raise taxes by eliminating what
is known as a “Flat-Tax.”

00:08:21.839 --> 00:08:27.229
Look, in Lithuania, as well as in Estonia
and Latvia, the tax systems are really simple.

00:08:27.229 --> 00:08:32.039
In Lithuania's case there is a flat tax, a
flat tax rate of 15% for people and companies.

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:37.461
That is to say that a Lithuanian who makes
10,000€ pays 1,500€ in taxes and the one

00:08:37.461 --> 00:08:43.209
who makes ten times more, 100,000€, also
pays 10 times more: 15,000€.

00:08:43.209 --> 00:08:47.371
Raising taxes in this way sort of goes against
the foreign investment and higher salaries

00:08:47.371 --> 00:08:51.130
plan, and this raise will directly harm both
of these things.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.730
And by the way, corporate taxes aren't as
low as they may seem:

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:59.720
If we add up the social security contributions
and other taxes, companies pay up more than

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.740
40% of their gross profits in taxes, according
to the World Bank.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.130
And now we have to point out the issue of
Russia and NATO.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:12.850
The previous Lithuanian governments (and the
current is no exception) have insisted on

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:18.220
scaring the population, and also the rest
of the world, with the Russian threat, saying

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.300
the country is constantly in danger...

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:27.050
OK, while it is true these threats are real,
it’s not really a brilliant way to instil

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:29.470
confidence in potential investors in the country…

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:34.690
And finally, and maybe the most important
of all, there is a cultural matter that affects

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:35.690
the entire country.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:40.410
So, if there is this brain drain doing on…
wouldn't it be logical to try to compensate

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:43.460
that loss by bringing young professionals
from other countries?

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:45.910
&nbsp;
Well, that’s a bit of a problem because

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.940
this country is also a bit suspicious about
immigration.

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:52.149
Most of these countries are a bit nationalist…

00:09:52.149 --> 00:09:54.209
&nbsp;
Anyway, if anything is for certain, it is

00:09:54.209 --> 00:09:58.680
that Lithuania as well as Estonia and Latvia
have done their homework and their policies

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:02.720
are certainly much more efficient that the
ones that predominate in the rest of Europe.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.600
Remember, in just 25 years they have managed
to become rich countries with high standards

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:08.600
of living.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.750
Well, now we’d like to know what you think:
Do you think these countries will finally

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.630
be able to stop this brain drain?

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:16.550
We are looking forward to hearing what you
think in the commenst section below.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:20.399
